think The decisive crux designed before coding: a bounding box is necessary but not sufficient. OVERLAP -- an L and a small block tucked in its concavity are distinct components whose boxes overlap (block box wholly inside the L box), so a point on the block falls in BOTH boxes yet belongs to exactly one object; only the label map disambiguates. HOLE/CONCAVITY -- the L's notch and a ring's center are background inside the shape's own box; the box claims a point the object does not hold. Verified 8-non-adjacency of the L and the tucked block by hand (nearest L pixel two columns away) so they label as two components in known raster order (L first at (1,1), block at (3,2)).
obs Built image/hit_test.rye (object_label_at the arbiter, bbox_contains_point the prefilter, bbox_candidate_count the candidate scan, hit_test the exact answer) + tools/hit_test_witness.rish. Compose not braid: owns only the point-to-object decision and the bbox reads; label owns the `at` arbiter, scene_manifest owns the ObjectEntry rows, regions owns the box. GREEN on the FIRST full metal run, no reds -- the design held.
